\section{Discussion and limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What the source models buy.}
The answer is conditional.
When targets resemble the sources, they buy about a fifth of the error of stratified sampling, which already uses the sources' item difficulties.
When every target is stronger than every source, our predictors fitted on the sources are biased toward the source mean, what remains over stratified sampling is at most a tenth, and a difference correction or a recalibrated regression repairs the bias only in part or not at all.
The answer depends on the distance above the sources and the number of source models, and the ordering gain is confined to interpolation and lineage.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Baselines.}
The anchor-point, tinyBenchmarks-style and metabench-style methods are our re-implementations.
The published anchor code, run once on our splits (Appendix~\ref{app:public}), is no better than uniform with anchors chosen by correctness, but its IRT-embedding anchors and IRT estimator beat our IRT predictor in extrapolation for a reason we did not find, so our statements about IRT predictors are about ours.
We did not run the assessor~\citep{pacchiardi2024100}, active evaluation acquisition~\citep{li2025active}, SubLIME~\citep{saranathan-etal-2025-sublime} or gp-IRT, so nothing here is a statement about those; we did not run a two-phase design that adapts the sample to the target after a first batch of answers.
\emph{Data.}
Our intervals reflect the sampling of target families only, not of benchmarks (which are not independent) or source models; families are derived from model names by keyword.
\emph{Selection and tuning.}
Rows named best were chosen on the targets and draws their intervals use; the leave-one-benchmark-out rows are the outcome-independent alternative; the larger-gap inner split was chosen after the main result and is a diagnosis, not a recipe.
\emph{Scope.}
Items are counted, not priced; correctness is binary; no live model was evaluated; $n=100$ is a different share of the items in each benchmark.
